# Dynamic programming in lambda plus merge, with no label: scored points
# of the label-free family.
#
# THE LANGUAGE is the lambda surface with the merge [T1, ..., Tn], with no
# label, and the judging applies as it is. The problems are those of contest_inheritance_dynamic.pl, written
# again in the design's patterns:
# - data are Scott encodings, one fixed eliminator per type: Booleans
#   \t. \f. t and \t. \f. f; unary numerals \z. \s. z and \z. \s. s n; binary
#   numerals with three variants, zero \z. \e. \o. z, even 2h (h > 0)
#   \z. \e. \o. e h and odd 2h + 1 \z. \e. \o. o h; lists \n. \c. n and
#   \n. \c. c h t; the two symbols \a. \b. a and \a. \b. b;
# - operations are curried functions, recursive by self-application: the
#   binary increment, plus and equality, and the symbols' equality;
# - each is bound once at the top of a program by a let, (\x. B) V, so a
#   program grows linearly, and an API is one code wherever it is used.
# ONLY BOOLEANS ARE MERGED. A merge feeds the same argument to every
# operand, so plus on a merge of numerals would mix the operands' bits, as
# it did in the labeled family (contest_inheritance_dynamic.pl, subset
# sum): {3, 5} + 7 read as {8, 10, 12, 14}. A search merges the Church
# Booleans of its branches, and the test reads true when any branch is
# true.
#
# THE PROBLEMS, each with the Prolog reference of
# contest_dynamic_references.pl (the usual algorithm, the complexity the
# problem expects):
# - grid paths: paths(r, c) = paths(r - 1, c) + paths(r, c - 1), 1 on an
#   edge, on unary coordinates with a binary count; the test compares
#   paths(n, n) with a claimed count;
# - subset sum: search_k(acc) merges search_(k+1)(acc) and
#   search_(k+1)(acc + a_k), and search_n(acc) is acc == target; the target
#   is the total of the first n odd primes, reached only by taking every
#   item;
# - longest common subsequence as a decision, lcs(left, right, need):
#   true when need is zero, false when a string is empty, and otherwise
#   the merge of leaving out left's head, leaving out right's head, and,
#   when the heads are equal, leaving out both with need - 1; the need is
#   the reference's length.
# The sizes follow the family's rule, n, 2n and 4n, with a
# frontier beyond: grid paths and LCS at 8, 16, 32 and 64, subset sum at
# 4, 8 and 16.
#
# WITHOUT LABELS THE PROBLEMS ARE EASIER TO SHARE. Each API is let bound
# once, so a recursive call such as `self self r' c` is spelled the same
# wherever it is reached, and plain tabling shares the subproblems of grid
# paths and LCS, which the labeled family's spellings hid: there every
# reading but the record reading was stopped at size 8, and the record
# reading at 8 as well. Subset sum stays exponential: a sum reached by
# different additions is spelled differently, and the reference
# deduplicates by value.
#
# READINGS (2026-09-28, CPU 2 under contest_limited.sh, a fresh process
# per reading, the intended template through claims_answered/4, 10 s,
# 256 MB, 256 MB):
# - weak_head: grid 8 in 8604 ms; subset 4 and 8 in 29 and 595 ms, subset
#   16 stopped; LCS 8 stopped.
# - tabling(weak_head): grid 8, 16 and 32 in 138, 826 and 7799 ms, grid 64
#   stopped (10247 ms); LCS 8, 16 and 32 in 99, 578 and 4864 ms, LCS 64
#   stopped (10065 ms); subset 4 and 8 in 20 and 324 ms, subset 10 and 12
#   in 1376 and 5275 ms in a probe, subset 16 stopped. Each doubling costs
#   grid paths about 9 and LCS about 8, where the references cost 4;
#   subset sum costs about 2 per item.
# - records_routed(record_interned(tabling(weak_head)), weak_head): grid 8
#   in 8540 ms, LCS 8 in 102 ms, subset 8 in 360 ms, subset 16 stopped.
# THE FRONTIER: grid paths 64, LCS 64 and subset sum 16, read by none of
# the three.
# BEYOND THE FRONTIER (settings read grid paths 64 and subset sum 16): one
# step beyond each, grid paths 128 and
# subset sum 24. Readings (2026-09-28, CPU 2 under contest_limited.sh,
# point_claims/2, the family's limits): weak_head and tabling(weak_head)
# stopped on both, grid paths 128 in 5856 and 5868 ms, subset sum 24 in
# 6645 and 6775 ms.
# LIMITS: the family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - dynamic programming, each subproblem solved once: R. Bellman, Dynamic
#   Programming, Princeton University Press 1957.
# - reached from the recursive definitions by memoisation or tabling: D.
#   Michie, "Memo" Functions and Machine Learning, Nature 1968,
#   doi:10.1038/218019a0; W. Chen, D. S. Warren, Tabled Evaluation with
#   Delaying for General Logic Programs, J. ACM 1996,
#   doi:10.1145/227595.227597.
# - longest common subsequence in quadratic time: R. A. Wagner, M. J.
#   Fischer, The String-to-String Correction Problem, J. ACM 1974,
#   doi:10.1145/321796.321811; D. S. Hirschberg, A Linear Space Algorithm
#   for Computing Maximal Common Subsequences, CACM 1975,
#   doi:10.1145/360825.360861.

#   grid_point(?Point, ?Size, ?Claimed): paths(Size, Size) == Claimed. The
